section: platform
title: The Palette platform
about: |
  What Palette itself does with a build: the validate routes it has and does not call, the
  states that hide an error, the webhook of the pivot, and the answers of its API.
rules:
- id: edge-agent-version
  title: The Palette agent of each edge host is as new as the Palette release
  engines:
  - edge
  tier: palette
  severity: warn
  status: inferred
  when: .release != null
  what: |
    The compatibility matrix pairs each Palette release with an agent (Stylus) release, such as
    4.10.16 with 4.10.4. Palette sets no floor on the agent version at create. A host with an
    agent from an older minor release misses the features that the profile and the release
    expect. The docs gate some features on the agent version. The check compares the
    major.minor of `status.health.agentVersion` with the release given; it is a reading of the
    matrix, not a run. It reads each host of each pool, one request for each.
  check:
    kind: palette
    method: GET
    each: .edgeHosts
    path: /v1/edgehosts/{{ $item.host.hostUid }}
    expr: |
      def mm: split(".") | .[:2] | map(sub("[^0-9].*$"; "") | if . == "" then 0 else tonumber end);
      .release as $rel
      | [ .responses[] | .item.host.hostUid as $u | .response as $r
        | if ($r.ok | not) then "Palette cannot read edge host \($u): \($r.body.code // $r.status): \($r.body.message // "")"
          else ($r.body.status.health.agentVersion // $r.body.spec.version // "") as $a
            | if $a == "" then "edge host \($u) reports no agent version"
              else ($a | mm) as $av | ($rel | mm) as $rv
                | if ($av[0] < $rv[0]) or ($av[0] == $rv[0] and ($av[1] // 0) < ($rv[1] // 0)) then "edge host \($u) runs agent \($a), and Palette is \($rel): the compatibility matrix pairs each Palette release with an agent release, and an older agent misses the features of the profile; upgrade the host first" else empty end
              end
          end ]
  reproduce:
  - 'for h in {{ [ .edgeHosts[].host.hostUid ] | join(" ") }}; do curl -s -H "ApiKey: $PALETTE_API_KEY" -H "ProjectUid: $PALETTE_PROJECT_UID" "${PALETTE_API:-https://api.spectrocloud.com}/v1/edgehosts/$h" | jq -r ''[.metadata.uid, .status.health.agentVersion, .spec.version] | @tsv''; done'
  fix:
    owner: palette
    text: Warn in the validate route when the agent is older than the release, with the matrix as the source.
  evidence:
  - kind: doc
    ref: https://docs.spectrocloud.com/clusters/edge/edge-compatibility-matrix/
    note: Palette release to agent release, such as 4.10.16 to 4.10.4
  - kind: source
    note: status.health.agentVersion
  tests:
  - fault: fixtures/edge/agent-old.json
    clean: fixtures/edge/clean.json
  - fault: fixtures/edge/agent-old-second-pool.json
- id: pcg-running
  title: The PCG of the cloud account is Running and active
  engines:
  - maas
  - vsphere
  tier: palette
  severity: fail
  status: source
  when: ((.account.metadata.annotations.overlordUid // "") != "") and ((.account.metadata.annotations.overlordType // "") != "system")
  what: |
    A MAAS or vSphere cloud account links to its private cloud gateway (PCG) through the
    annotation `overlordUid`. The create refuses a PCG that is not `Running`: "Unable to deploy
    the cluster as the gateway is in ... state". The vSphere validate route refuses an unhealthy
    one too. The account validate and the property routes need `isActive`. A system gateway of a
    self-hosted Palette skips the check. `GET /v1/overlords/{uid}` gives the state, `isActive`,
    and the health.
  check:
    kind: palette
    method: GET
    path: /v1/overlords/{{ .account.metadata.annotations.overlordUid }}
    expr: |
      .account.metadata.annotations.overlordUid as $ol | .account.metadata.name as $acc
      | if (.response.ok | not) then ["the PCG \($ol) of cloud account \($acc) is not there: \(.response.body.code // .response.status): \(.response.body.message // ""); Palette refuses the create with ClusterDeploymentFailureForMissingOverlord"]
        else (.response.body.status // {}) as $s | (.response.body.spec // {}) as $sp | (.response.body.metadata.name // $ol) as $name
          | if ($sp.isSystem // false) then []
            else [ ( if ($s.state // "") != "Running" then "the PCG \($name) is \($s.state // "in no state"), not Running: Palette refuses the create (\"should be in Running state\")" else empty end ),
                   ( if (($s.isActive // false) | not) then "the PCG \($name) is not active: the account validate and the property routes need an active PCG" else empty end ),
                   ( if (($s.health.state // "Healthy") | ascii_downcase) != "healthy" then "the PCG \($name) health is \($s.health.state): the vSphere validate route refuses an unhealthy PCG (OverlordClusterUnHealthyState)" else empty end ) ]
            end
        end
  reproduce:
  - 'curl -s -H "ApiKey: $PALETTE_API_KEY" -H "ProjectUid: $PALETTE_PROJECT_UID" "${PALETTE_API:-https://api.spectrocloud.com}/v1/overlords/{{ .account.metadata.annotations.overlordUid }}" | jq ''{name: .metadata.name, state: .status.state, active: .status.isActive, ready: .status.isReady, health: .status.health.state, system: .spec.isSystem}'''
  fix:
    owner: palette
    text: Show the PCG state next to the cloud account in the console, and in the validate route of MAAS.
  evidence:
  - kind: source
    note: Palette refuses a PCG that is not Running, unless the PCG is a system gateway
  - kind: source
    note: OverlordClusterNotReady and OverlordClusterUnHealthyState in the vSphere validate route
  - kind: source
    note: the cloud account carries the PCG uid in metadata.annotations.overlordUid; overlordType system marks the system gateway
  - kind: doc
    ref: https://docs.spectrocloud.com/clusters/pcg/architecture/
    note: all Palette deployed clusters use the PCG during the creation and deletion phase
  tests:
  - fault: fixtures/vsphere/pcg-pending.json
    clean: fixtures/vsphere/clean.json
  - fault: fixtures/vsphere/pcg-missing.json
    clean: fixtures/maas/clean.json
- id: auto-cleanup-never
  title: A delete that makes no progress moves to the cleanup path
  engines:
  - all
  tier: race
  severity: fail
  status: source
  what: |
    Palette gives a delete to the pilot only while `DeProvisionDone` is absent. After that, the
    agent in the cluster runs the delete. Palette moves a delete to the cleanup cluster only
    when it cannot reach the target cluster for 10 minutes. A
    deleting cluster with a live agent that makes no progress stays where it is; no step of the
    source moves it. In one run a second delete request moved it, by a path that is not in the
    source. No check before a build finds it; the watch sees the state.
  fix:
    owner: palette
    text: Move a delete that makes no progress for a limit to the cleanup path.
  evidence:
  - kind: source
    note: the cleanup cluster waits 10 minutes for the target cluster, then makes the objects again from Palette
  - kind: source
    note: a delete goes to the pilot only while DeProvisionDone is absent
  - kind: run
    ref: a live run
